# Example: referencing coding-guidelines from a project

Copy the relevant block into the project's `CLAUDE.md` (or `AGENTS.md`) and
adjust the path. Two ways to reference the repo:

- **Checkout / submodule** — clone this repo next to (or inside) the project
  and point at files with `@` imports, so the content is loaded automatically.
- **Path reference** — name the files and let the agent read them on demand.
  Prefer this when you want to keep the always-on context small.

---

## Variant A: Go service, guidelines checked out as a sibling directory

```markdown
# Coding guidelines

This project follows ../coding-guidelines. Load before writing code:

@../coding-guidelines/principles.md
@../coding-guidelines/languages/go.md
```

## Variant B: React + TypeScript app, loaded on demand

```markdown
# Coding guidelines

This project follows the rules in ~/engineer/coding-guidelines.
Before writing or reviewing code, read:

- ~/engineer/coding-guidelines/principles.md   (always)
- ~/engineer/coding-guidelines/languages/typescript.md
- ~/engineer/coding-guidelines/frontend/react.md

Rules there are normative (MUST/SHOULD/NEVER). If a project-local rule in
this file conflicts with the guidelines, this file wins — note the conflict
in your response.
```

## Variant C: git submodule pinned to a commit

```bash
git submodule add https://example.com/you/coding-guidelines docs/guidelines
```

```markdown
# Coding guidelines

@docs/guidelines/principles.md
@docs/guidelines/languages/rust.md
```

Pin the submodule and bump it deliberately, so guideline changes arrive as
reviewable diffs instead of silently changing agent behavior.
